  {
    "test_id": "A5",
    "category": "Factual",
    "question": "What is network slicing?",
    "selected_release": "18",
    "selected_specifications": [
      "TS 23.501"
    ],
    "actual_behavior": "Network slicing is a logical network that provides specific network capabilities and network characteristics [CHUNK_ID: TS_23.501_18.0.0_80]. A Network Slice instance is defined within a PLMN or within an SNPN and includes the Core Network Control Plane and User Plane Network Functions, as well as at least one of the following: the NG-RAN, the N3IWF or TNGF functions to the non-3GPP Access Network, or the W-AGF function to the Wireline Access Network [CHUNK_ID: TS_23.501_18.0.0_788]. Network slices may differ in supported features and network functions optimisations, and may have different S-NSSAIs with different Slice/Service Types or Slice Differentiators [CHUNK_ID: TS_23.501_18.0.0_789].",
    "retrieved_sources": [
